<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#groupCloseToken> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The literal that closes a group: END_OBJECT, End, or the bracket ]. For sectionHeaded it is the closing bracket of the header rather than a separate record, since a section is ended by the next one."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#groupCloseToken> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#groupOpenToken> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The literal that marks a record as opening a group: the key OBJECT, the value Begin, or the bracket [ -- read according to groupingStyle."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#groupOpenToken> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#groupingStyle> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The grouping convention that determines how opening and closing records establish named nested groups."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#groupingStyle> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasArgument> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Ordered rdf:List of HEL expression strings, the positional arguments for the parameterised Struct this resource names as a type: on a Field, for its bddo:dataType; on a DataTypeRule, for its bddo:ruleDataType; on a DispatchArm, for its bddo:armDataType. A bddo:dispatchDefault has nowhere to state arguments, so it never names a parameterised struct. Each is evaluated in the CALLER's context -- the struct containing the field -- at the point the field is read."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasArgument> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasConditionalDataType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The ordered list of conditional datatype rules used to select how a field is parsed."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasConditionalDataType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasConditionalDataType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasConditionalEndianness> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Ordered rdf:List of EndiannessRule. The first rule whose condition holds selects the byte order for this Struct or Field and, as with bddo:endianness, for its descendants unless they override it. Use for formats that declare their own byte order, such as TIFF's 4949h/4D4Dh marker. Mutually exclusive with bddo:endianness."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasConditionalEndianness> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasConditionalEndianness> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasDispatchTable> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The keyed dispatch table that selects this field's effective type; exclusive with the ordered conditional datatype rules."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasDispatchTable> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasDispatchTable> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasEndianness> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Deprecated pre-release spelling of bddo:endianness, with the same meaning. A processor reading a description that states both uses bddo:endianness."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasEndianness> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. No shape of the family constrains it, and no global domain is inferred."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasEnumValue> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An enumeration member associating a raw value or mask with a symbol."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasEnumValue> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasEnumValue> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasField> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Ordered rdf:List of the members of a Struct: its Fields, and any LocalBindings among them, in the order they are processed."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasField> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasField> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasFixedValue> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The literal a parsed field is required to equal, such as a signature or fixed discriminator."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasFixedValue> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. No shape of the family constrains it, and no global domain is inferred."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasFixedValue> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasGrouping> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The nesting rule of a DelimitedRecords container. Absent means flat."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasGrouping> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasNamespaceBinding> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Binds the prefixes used in this document's paths. XML only, and not optional in practice: PDS4, DIMAP and SAFE are all namespaced, and an unprefixed path against a namespaced document silently matches nothing rather than failing."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasNamespaceBinding> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasParameter> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Ordered rdf:List of the bddo:Parameter resources of a parameterised Struct. Every place that names the struct as a type supplies exactly one argument per parameter, positionally, with bddo:hasArgument."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#hasParameter> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
