<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#json> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Paths are JSON POINTERS as defined by RFC 6901: /object/member, array indices as /list/0 (0-based). Adopted rather than invented, so its escaping rules for '/' and '~' inside a member name are already specified and already implemented."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#json> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use this IRI as a controlled value where the profile or a shape accepts its declared type."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#json> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#key> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The key naming this Field within a KeyValueHeader. Replaces positional location: the Field takes the value of whichever record carries this key. A field of a flat KeyValueHeader that declares no bddo:key is located by its simple key instead."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#key> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#key> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#keyIsCaseInsensitive> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "If true, bddo:key matching ignores ASCII case. Default false."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#keyIsCaseInsensitive> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#keyIsCaseInsensitive> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#keyPath> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Locates a field inside nested groups, as group names and finally the key, joined by pathSeparator: 'IMAGE/BAND/NAME'. Mutually exclusive with bddo:key, which locates a field in a flat container -- a field cannot be found two ways, and a description that said both would leave a reader to guess which wins."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#keyPath> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#keyValueSeparator> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Byte sequence separating a key from its value within a record of a KeyValueHeader."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#keyValueSeparator> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#keyValueSeparator> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#keyedGroup> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A record whose KEY equals groupOpenToken opens a group, and its VALUE names it; a record whose key equals groupCloseToken closes the innermost open group. PVL/ODL: OBJECT = IMAGE ... END_OBJECT = IMAGE. The closing record MAY repeat the name; a description need not say so, because the name is already known from the opening record."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#keyedGroup> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use this IRI as a controlled value where the profile or a shape accepts its declared type."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#keyedGroup> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#latin1> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "ISO-8859-1 character encoding mapping each byte to the corresponding Latin-1 character value."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#latin1> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use this IRI as a controlled value where the profile or a shape accepts its declared type."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#latin1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#localExpression> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The HEL expression giving a LocalBinding's value, evaluated with the containing struct as instance when the binding's position in the hasField list is reached."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#localExpression> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#md5> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The MD5 message-digest identifier, producing a 128-bit digest; this term identifies a stored integrity algorithm and does not claim cryptographic security."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#md5> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use this IRI as a controlled value where the profile or a shape accepts its declared type."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#md5> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#namespaceIRI> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The namespace the prefix stands for, matched against the document's own declarations rather than against the prefix it happens to use -- a document may spell the same namespace with any prefix."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#namespaceIRI> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#namespacePrefix> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The prefix as used in this container's nodePath values. The empty string binds the DEFAULT namespace, which an XPath subset cannot otherwise reach."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#namespacePrefix> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#nodePath> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Locates the node supplying this field's value, read according to the container's treeSyntax. A path selecting no node leaves the field absent; a path selecting several is an error unless the field repeats, in which case the field's value is the selected nodes in document order."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#nodePath> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#numericBase> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Radix used to read an :asciiInteger field: 8, 10 or 16. Default 10."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#numericBase> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#numericBase> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#offsetBase> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The origin used to interpret a field's explicit byte displacement; it is not a spatial reference or a calibration offset."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#offsetBase> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#offsetBase> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#parameterType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Restricts the kind of value a Parameter accepts to one HEL value type: bddo:IntegerParameter, bddo:FloatParameter (an Integer argument is converted to Float), bddo:StringParameter, bddo:BytesParameter or bddo:BooleanParameter. Absent, the parameter accepts any scalar value, Null included."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#parameterType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#parentStart> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Offset reference at the beginning of the containing parsed structure."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#parentStart> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use this IRI as a controlled value where the profile or a shape accepts its declared type."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#parentStart> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#pathSeparator> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The separator used in this container's keyPath values. Default '/'. Declared on the container rather than repeated per field, and needed because a format whose group names may contain '/' has to choose something else."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#pathSeparator> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#quoteChar> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Byte that quotes a value, allowing it to contain the field or record delimiter. A doubled quote inside a quoted value denotes a literal quote."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#quoteChar> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#quoteChar> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#recordDelimiter> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Byte sequence separating records. Default 0A (LF); a CR LF pair is also accepted when this is 0A."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#recordDelimiter> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#recordDelimiter> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#regionScope> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Seek scope confining an offset-addressed read to the innermost bounded region; the default."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#regionScope> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use this IRI as a controlled value where the profile or a shape accepts its declared type."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#regionScope> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#repeatCount> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The fixed number of occurrences of a repeated field. Zero denotes an empty repetition."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#repeatCount> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#repeatCount> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#repeatCountFromExpression> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The HEL expression that computes the number of occurrences of a repeated field."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#repeatCountFromExpression> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#repeatCountFromExpression> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#repeatCountFromField> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The already available field whose numeric value determines a repetition count."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#repeatCountFromField> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. No shape of the family constrains it, and no global domain is inferred."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#repeatCountFromField> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#repeatUntil> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A HEL termination condition for a repeated field, evaluated according to the processing model's repetition rules."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#repeatUntil> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#repeatUntil> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#rootKeyFromField> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "After this Field is parsed, its value is also bound in the ROOT parsing context under the key given by the named sibling Field's parsed value, if that key is not already bound (first occurrence wins). Lets a later record address an earlier one through its discriminator -- `root.<key>.<field>` -- when the earlier record sits inside a repeated sequence that HEL cannot search. A neutral generalisation of the chunk-type aliasing that several chunked container formats need."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#rootKeyFromField> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#rootKeyFromField> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#ruleDataType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The datatype or structure selected when a conditional datatype rule matches."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#ruleDataType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#ruleDataType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#ruleEndianness> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The byte order selected by an EndiannessRule."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#ruleEndianness> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#ruleEndianness> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#sectionHeaded> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A record consisting of groupOpenToken, a name, and groupCloseToken opens a group and implicitly closes the previous one; the last runs to the end of the container. INI-style [Section] headers. Nesting is therefore FLAT ONE DEEP -- sections do not contain sections -- which is what the style actually permits, not a limitation of this model."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#sectionHeaded> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use this IRI as a controlled value where the profile or a shape accepts its declared type."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#sectionHeaded> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#seekScope> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Whether an offset-addressed read is confined to its bounded region or resolved against the whole stream."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#seekScope> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#seekScope> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#separatedBy> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The element extent of a field -- repeated or scalar -- whose value is a number written as text, separated rather than sized or terminated: each element is preceded and followed by a run of separator bytes, and the run belongs to the element that owns it so the byte sequence can be rebuilt exactly. The only defined value is \"whitespace\" (space, tab, CR, LF). A separator is a RUN and a terminator is a SEQUENCE: between two delimiters of a delimited table an empty value is meaningful, whereas an empty run is not a value at all. Permitted only on a field whose data type is a number written as text. Because the LAST element's trailing run consumes every separator byte up to the field's own extent (or end of stream), a field declaring bddo:separatedBy must either be the last field read from its enclosing region, or be immediately followed by data that does not itself begin with a separator byte (space, tab, CR, LF) -- otherwise the trailing run consumes bytes that belong to the following field."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#separatedBy> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#separatedBy> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#sha1> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The SHA-1 message-digest identifier, producing a 160-bit digest; this term does not claim suitability for collision-resistant security uses."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#sha1> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use this IRI as a controlled value where the profile or a shape accepts its declared type."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#sha1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#sha256> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The SHA-256 message-digest identifier, producing a 256-bit digest of the covered byte sequence."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#sha256> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use this IRI as a controlled value where the profile or a shape accepts its declared type."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#sha256> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#size> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A fixed byte extent declared for a field or bounded structure. This is a byte count, not a bit width or repetition count."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#size> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#size> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#sizeFromExpression> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A HEL expression whose result supplies the byte extent in the applicable parsing context."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#sizeFromExpression> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#sizeFromExpression> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#sizeFromField> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A reference to an already available field whose parsed numeric value supplies the byte extent."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#sizeFromField> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#sizeFromField> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#sizeToEndOfStream> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "When true, consumes the remaining bytes of the applicable bounded stream region. It does not bypass an enclosing bound."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#sizeToEndOfStream> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#sizeToEndOfStream> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#skipRecords> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Number of leading records to discard before parsing, e.g. a CSV column-name row. Default 0."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#skipRecords> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#skipRecords> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#sqliteVarint> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "SQLite's variable-length integer: one to nine BIG-endian bytes, high bit as continuation for the first eight, the ninth contributing all eight bits. A different scheme from LEB128, not a dialect of it; used by SQLite-backed formats (GeoPackage, MBTiles, Rasterlite)."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#sqliteVarint> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use this IRI as a controlled value where the profile or a shape accepts its declared type."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#sqliteVarint> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#streamEnd> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Offset reference at the end of the applicable bounded stream; interpretation follows the offset rules of the processing model."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#streamEnd> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use this IRI as a controlled value where the profile or a shape accepts its declared type."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#streamEnd> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#streamScope> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Seek scope resolving an offset-addressed read against the whole stream, suspending the region bound for that read."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#streamScope> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use this IRI as a controlled value where the profile or a shape accepts its declared type."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#streamScope> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#streamStart> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Offset reference at the beginning of the applicable bounded stream."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#streamStart> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use this IRI as a controlled value where the profile or a shape accepts its declared type."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#streamStart> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#string> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Variable-length primitive text datatype; its byte extent and character encoding are supplied by the field/profile."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#string> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use this IRI as a controlled value where the profile or a shape accepts its declared type."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#string> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#syncOnMarker> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A byte marker used to locate synchronization points when reading a repeated structured sequence."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#syncOnMarker> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#syncOnMarker> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#terminator> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The byte sequence that terminates a variable-length field. Delimiter handling follows the processing contract rather than an inferred text encoding."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#terminator> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#terminator> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#treeSyntax> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The grammar of a TreeDocument. Required, because it decides how every nodePath in the container is read."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#treeSyntax> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#trimNull> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "For a fixed-size (padded) string field, the emitted value ends at the first 0x00 byte within the field's width."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#trimNull> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#trimNull> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#trimWhitespace> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "If true, leading and trailing whitespace is removed from every key and value. Default false."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#trimWhitespace> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#trimWhitespace> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#uint16> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A 16-bit unsigned integer primitive datatype. Byte order is not fixed on this datatype; the field and processing context determine it."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#uint16> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use this IRI as a controlled value where the profile or a shape accepts its declared type."@en .
