<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#uint8> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use this IRI as a controlled value where the profile or a shape accepts its declared type."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#uint8> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#usesStruct> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Deprecated pre-release template reference: the subject Struct's effective field list is the named Struct's fields followed by its own. There is no single replacement term; new descriptions state the fields directly, or compose with a Field whose bddo:dataType is the other Struct (which nests rather than splices)."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#usesStruct> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. No shape of the family constrains it, and no global domain is inferred."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#utf16be> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "UTF-16 character encoding with each 16-bit code unit stored in big-endian byte order."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#utf16be> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use this IRI as a controlled value where the profile or a shape accepts its declared type."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#utf16be> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#utf16le> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "UTF-16 character encoding with each 16-bit code unit stored in little-endian byte order."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#utf16le> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use this IRI as a controlled value where the profile or a shape accepts its declared type."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#utf16le> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#utf8> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "UTF-8 character encoding for textual bytes; a variable number of bytes may represent one Unicode scalar value."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#utf8> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use this IRI as a controlled value where the profile or a shape accepts its declared type."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#utf8> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#validIf> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A HEL boolean expression that MUST hold once this field is parsed; if it evaluates false the parse fails with a validation error. Use for post-parse constraints such as a magic number or a bound on a count. Richer, graph-level constraints belong in SHACL at the semantic layer."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#validIf> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#validIf> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#valueFromExpression> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A HEL expression giving the value of a derived, zero-byte field. The field consumes no bytes; its computed value is placed in the parse context under the field's name so later sizing, offset, presence, mapping and validation expressions may reference it. A field with bddo:valueFromExpression declares no bddo:dataType and no sizing, offset or bit-length; the result's type is that of the HEL expression."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#valueFromExpression> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#valueFromExpression> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#valuedGroup> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The mirror image: a record whose VALUE equals groupOpenToken opens a group, and its KEY names it. ERMapper: CoordinateSpace Begin ... CoordinateSpace End."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#valuedGroup> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use this IRI as a controlled value where the profile or a shape accepts its declared type."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#valuedGroup> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#varint> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Signed base-128 varint: LEB128 carrying a zigzag-encoded value, so small negatives stay short -- n maps to (n << 1) XOR (n >> 63). Protobuf's sint32/sint64. Distinct from reading an unsigned varint and reinterpreting the bits, which is protobuf's int32 and sign-extends instead."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#varint> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use this IRI as a controlled value where the profile or a shape accepts its declared type."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#varint> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#varuint> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Unsigned base-128 varint (LEB128): 7 value bits per byte, little-endian, the high bit set on every byte but the last. Protobuf's plain varint."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#varuint> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use this IRI as a controlled value where the profile or a shape accepts its declared type."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#varuint> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#whitespaceSeparated> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "If true, values are separated by a RUN of one or more whitespace bytes (space, tab, CR, LF) rather than by a fixed bddo:fieldDelimiter. A run of any length separates exactly two values, so consecutive whitespace never yields an empty value, and leading or trailing whitespace on a record is ignored. This is what column-aligned ASCII grids need -- 'ncols        100', or a row of numbers padded to line up -- which a fixed delimiter cannot express. Mutually exclusive with bddo:fieldDelimiter. When bddo:recordDelimiter is also absent the content is one flat stream of whitespace-separated values rather than rows, which is how an ASCII grid whose rows wrap across lines is read; the grid's shape then comes from the field's dlv:DataLayout or repeat count, not from line breaks."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#whitespaceSeparated> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#whitespaceSeparated> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#xml> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Paths are ABBREVIATED XPATH: child steps, attribute steps (@name), and positional predicates ([n], 1-based). A deliberately small subset -- no axes, no functions, no comparison predicates -- because a description states where a value IS, and a path that can compute is a query engine's problem rather than a description's."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#xml> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use this IRI as a controlled value where the profile or a shape accepts its declared type."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#xml> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#xsdType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The RDF literal datatype produced when a value of this DataType is emitted."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#xsdType> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Use in physical binary-format descriptions. Extents and offsets describe bytes unless a term explicitly specifies bits; HEL evaluation and failure behavior follow the processing specification. Use it as a predicate. Applicable SHACL paths and their focus-node scopes are listed below; no additional global domain is inferred from that usage."@en .
<https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo#xsdType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://hexplain.io/ns/bddo> .
